\section{Creating the object and adding responses}{


First, use \link[=cat_start]{cat_start()} to create the object by
providing the metadata (aka the description of expected responses). Once this
is done, the object describes your elicitation but contains no responses.
Then use \link[=cat_add_data]{cat_add_data()} to add the response data.

Save the result of each call using \verb{<-}. For example,
\code{my_elicitation <- cat_add_data(my_elicitation, responses, topic = "topic1")}
updates the topic \code{topic1} of the \code{elic_cat} object called \code{my_elicitation}
with the responses stored in the data file \code{responses}. Use these functions
to create and update the object rather than editing its contents manually, so
that the package can check the information.
}

\section{Why keep metadata and data together?}{


The metadata tell the package what responses are expected.
This allows \link{elicitr} to check whether data subsequently added in
the \code{elic_cat} object fit the expectations. This way, data verification
happens automatically instead of having to be done manually by the user.

For example, \link{elicitr} knows that the sum of the estimates provided
by an expert for all categories must be 100 or to 1 for each option.
\link{elicitr} will check that data loaded in the \code{elic_cat} object fit
that description and provide errors or warnings if that is not the case.

Keeping this information together means you do not need to enter it again
for every analysis. You can pass the same object to
\link[=cat_get_data]{cat_get_data()},
\link[=cat_sample_data]{cat_sample_data()},
\link[=cat_summary]{cat_summary()}, and
\link[=plot.cat_sample]{plot()} to retrieve responses, generate samples,
summarise results, and create plots.
}

